WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.010
- First the good news:

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:03.940
cloud development strategies mean more

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.600
and faster deployments.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.060
Now the bad news:

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.130
more and faster deployments
can impact your IT operations

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:13.060
team, increasing the time
to resolve incidents.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.150
That potentially means unhappy
customers and more resources

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:19.210
dedicated to keeping your
systems running smoothly.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.350
Hi, I'm Dan Kehn from IBM Cloud®.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.260
So what's your ops team to do?

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.200
I'll cover that question in
two quick demonstrations,

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.850
but first let's briefly review the phases

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.940
of incident resolution, then
I'll explain how automation

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.393
and AI can help.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.570
Mean time to resolution
is the big picture.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:41.300
It covers everything from the
time the problem has started

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.200
until it's finally resolved.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.740
The longer it takes to resolve,

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.993
the worse the impact to your organization.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.300
Some parts of resolution
time are consistent,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.320
like mean time to fix, or MTTF.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.340
Others vary significantly,
like mean time to identify,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.910
or MTTI, which can run from hours to days.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.470
That's because it relies
on operators' experience

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.683
and knowledge of the system relationships.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.350
To help lower MTTI, IBM
Cloud Pak® for Watson AIOps

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.960
comes with built-in anomaly
identification strategies

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.900
that use machine learning.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.300
Of course, machine learning
works best when there's lots

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.610
of varied, high-quality data.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:21.420
That's why Cloud Pak for
Watson AIOps consumes data

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.640
from many sources, it
then uses AI to discover

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.950
relationships across these
different data sources

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.773
and weigh possible causes.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:32.380
This unsupervised learning
reduces the time needed

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.200
to realize the value of AI,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.260
so instead of requiring
extensive training,

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:37.820
you can get started right away,

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.113
with out-of-the-box, pre-trained models.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.730
Okay, with that intro out of the way,

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:45.970
I'd like to walk you through
two incidents and how

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:48.480
Cloud Pak for Watson
AIOps can help you resolve

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.690
them more quickly.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:52.410
The first I call lucky day.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.520
With the smart search of prior solutions,

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.100
you close the incident using
documented steps in a run book.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.500
The second, which unfortunately
happens more often

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.870
than we'd like to admit,
I call not-so-lucky day.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.400
This is an undiscovered
problem that requires sifting

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.250
through misbehaving servers
and confirming the root cause.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.470
Imagine you're at lunch and a notification

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.420
from Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps comes in.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.070
You double click to check it out.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.570
In the problem summary, you
recognize one of the services

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:23.350
you monitor, so you decide to investigate.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.120
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps
shows you a summary view

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.490
based on data gathered directly
from your app monitoring

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.620
logs and from integrated
tools like PagerDuty, Splunk,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.330
and ServiceNow.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.040
The chat entry shows you
several different fields.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.210
First, the impacted application,

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.190
the train ticket application.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.820
Next, a hypothesized
storage of the problem,

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.790
the ticket info service.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.180
It also shows you incident
severity and status.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.010
Finally, you can see a summary
ticket that was automatically

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.763
generated by Watson AIOps.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.530
Two key questions for resolving
a problem are what changed

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.520
and what happened nearby?

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.630
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps groups events

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.780
that represent change and a topology map

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.340
that represents nearby connected services.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.200
That is what changed,
how recently it changed,

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.220
and how frequently it
changed gives you hints

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.260
about the source of the problem.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.070
With smart topology and an
understanding of the context,

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.120
you now know where to start.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.160
Okay, I've shown you how
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.650
provides a summary of key
diagnostic information,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.430
but it also helps consolidate information

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.983
from multiple tools across
different data silos.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.640
This view shows a summary of
the anomalies that underlie

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:34.870
the problem report.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:37.040
This helps reduce information overload

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.070
and avoid notification flooding.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.180
It also saves you from the
hassle of chasing problems

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.453
across different tools.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.960
Now that you have a better
understanding of the incident,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.810
you take action to resolve it.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.750
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps
has identified similar tickets

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.680
based on data interpreted with
natural language processing

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.810
and pre-trained AI models.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.270
This can help you quickly
identify relevant tickets

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.310
with possible solutions.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.440
By pinpointing specific
actions that your team

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.890
has taken in the past, you
don't have to deal with the

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.350
tedium of manually reviewing
a list of prior tickets.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.940
You confirm the run book
matches the current problem

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.700
and resolve the incident.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:13.533
Excellent.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.010
And the prior investigation, we got lucky.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.210
The problem had been resolved once before,

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.480
so we only had to execute a run book.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.130
But what if it wasn't so easy?

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.270
And that's where AI and
machine learning really shine.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.230
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps
consumes huge volumes

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.550
of your system data,

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.080
structured data like
configuration topology,

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.960
semi-structured data like
logs and ticket information,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.770
even unstructured data
like commit comments.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.619
Based on this data, it learns
what normal looks like so

00:04:41.619 --> 00:04:46.019
it can alert you when metrics
are outside expected bounds.

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:48.350
But the metric manager in
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.890
doesn't rely on fixed threshold tracking.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.520
This avoids the trap where
a high fixed threshold

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.680
generates too few alerts and real problems

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.550
are ignored until they become serious

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.900
or low threshold generates too many alerts

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.300
and your operators simply tune them out.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:05.690
Instead, Cloud Pak for Watson
AIOps uses machine learning

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.390
to understand what's normal behavior

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:10.440
for key performance metrics
and automatically sets

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.750
adaptive threshold based on
actual system experience.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.250
Now let's look at how
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.510
helps you with your not-so-lucky day.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.270
It's later in the week and
you're handling another incident.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.580
This time it's a claims
app and users are reporting

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.490
really bad response times.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.370
You start your investigation by opening

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:28.593
the events dashboard.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.530
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps
recognizes many data sources

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:34.720
for event correlation.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.390
For example, data from Log
DNA, Serves Now, PagerDuty

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.200
and hundreds of other integrations.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.090
The dashboard groups related events based

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.090
on inferred associations like topology,

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.230
the time occurrence, and location.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.990
Let's take a closer look
at the event leading up

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.853
to the claims app slowing down.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.950
This metrics timeline can help you

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:58.080
with problem determination
and assess potential impacts.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.633
The green indicates
normal behavior over time.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:03.890
You can visualize baseline
performance compared

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.593
to the recently-captured data.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.020
The secondary view shows metrics

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.230
based on application observability.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.380
These are metrics
discovered and identified

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.330
as being related to the
app's response time.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:19.220
Here we see the disk
busy metric is unstable,

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.970
let's add it to the timeline
for further investigation.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.750
Now the primary review
shows that just before

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.640
the response time problems, the disk busy

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:28.870
for the storage service went up

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:32.150
to nearly 100% utilization
and it stayed there.

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:33.323
That's never good.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:36.700
Based on this brief analysis,
you know the data store

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.430
for the database was overloaded.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.380
It's a prime candidate for the root cause

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.320
of the app's slowdown.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.730
The next step is confirming
your analysis by checking

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:48.110
the service logs and then
proposing a proper solution.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:50.910
The analysis of the relationships
between these metrics

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.480
help you understand the
full scope of the problem.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.790
Once the fix is delivered,
you can say with confidence,

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:58.580
you've identified and
resolved the true cause.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.500
Okay, let's recap.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.380
When it comes to IT Ops,
it's better to be proactive

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:06.500
than constantly being forced
into a reactive posture.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.360
With dynamically-determined
rules, the data analysis

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.500
by Cloud Pak for Watson
AIOps helps you get

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.120
to resolution quicker,
potentially before your users

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:15.890
even notice a problem.

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:18.150
And you don't have to manage rules,

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.450
consider how they
interact with each other,

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:21.380
or worry about how rules should change

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.140
when the environment changes.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:25.480
What can automation mean to your company?

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:28.350
How about 25% more time
spent on work that drives

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:32.650
your business or reducing
manual labor costs by 50%?

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:34.000
Thanks for watching.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.230
If you'd like to see more
videos like this in the future,

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.290
please click like and subscribe.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:40.890
If you want to learn more about
Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps,

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.413
make sure to check out the
links in the description.

